{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>וְנָתַתִּי גִשְׁמֵיכֶם</b> — one must understand His saying ״וְנָתַתִּי״ with a waw of addition, when no other promise preceded this. According to what I wrote in the commentary on ״אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי״ — that there is an allusion in the word ״תֵּלֵכוּ״ — it is fitting to say ״וְנָתַתִּי״ with the addition of waw. This also resolves what one may further question: for from the plain sense of the verse it appears that the reward for engagement in Torah and for positive and negative commandments is <b>וְנָתַתִּי גִשְׁמֵיכֶם</b>; if so, the reward for a mitzvah is in this world, and a person would depart empty of reward for his service. By his precise choice to say <b>וְנָתַתִּי</b> with the addition of waw, he alerted us that other promises preceded — for the World to Come — and they are what I alluded to in the verse ״אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ״." "<b>Or</b> it may say: since the reward of the supernal world is spiritual, and its promises cannot be grasped in thought nor in speech, therefore he alluded by adding waw at the beginning of the matter to say that there is another reward — and He adds further to give ״גִּשְׁמֵיכֶם״. And he was precise in saying <b>גִּשְׁמֵיכֶם</b> — as though they are yours, not as payment for the reward of mitzvot and good deeds, but rather because through them the world is sustained through engagement in Torah etc., they earn them as their own. And perhaps it is for this reason that He saw fit and permitted the wealth of the nations to Israel (Bava Kamma 38a), for Israel acquired the world and all that is in it, and the nations are like the property of the children of Israel:" "<b>Further,</b> it intends to say that even though He gives ״גִּשְׁמֵיכֶם״ as a gift, He does not consider the thing as belonging to Him, but rather as though the thing is yours." "<b>And he was precise</b> in saying <b>וְנָתַתִּי</b> — that He, the Master, needs to perform the matter Himself, in order to bring down the drops of your rains in their time and according to the measure required, for He knows the time and the measure that the earth should drink and that will benefit it — which no one other than Him, blessed be He, can do. And so you will find that they said, of blessed memory (Leviticus Rabbah 35:3), and this is their language: ״A person lies on his bed and the Holy One, blessed be He, raises clouds.״" "<b>Further,</b> it intends in saying <b>גִשְׁמֵיכֶם</b> upon the entirety of sustenance — whether needed for the world, whether needed for the soil, whether needed for the souls — for all of them depend on being nourished, and there are physical dews and rains, and there are spiritual dews and rains as is known to those who know grace (יוֹדְעֵי חֵן), and concerning all of them He said ״וְנָתַתִּי גִשְׁמֵיכֶם״; and see what I wrote in parashat Behar Sinai." "<b>וְנָתְנָה</b> הָאָרֶץ וגו׳ — I have explained this in parashat Behar Sinai."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:44.397416785Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}